        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes a matrix that is 1 along a diagonal and 0 everywhere else. Square, with the main diagonal, it is the identity matrix: the matrix that changes nothing when you multiply by it, as 1 does for ordinary numbers.\n\nUse it as the starting point for a transformation you then modify, as the \"no change\" reference to compare a rotation or fit against, to add a little to a diagonal to steady an inversion, or (shifted off the main diagonal) as a matrix that moves each element one place along.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.eye <rows: int> <columns: int> <diagonal: int>\nDefaults: 3 rows, square, the main diagonal.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.eye 4\nnp.eye 3 4 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the matrix and sends it.\n\nrows (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many rows.\n\ncolumns (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many columns; 0 (the default) makes it square.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich diagonal holds the ones: 0 the main one, 1 the one just above it, -1 the one just below.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\neye out:\nThe matrix (32-bit floats).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nClicked at load: a 3 x 3 identity, and a 3 x 4 matrix with its ones one place to the right of the main diagonal. Change the numbers and click a button to see others.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diag makes a matrix with any values on the diagonal. t.eye is the PyTorch version. np.zeros and np.ones fill a whole matrix.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes a matrix that is 1 along a diagonal and 0 everywhere else. Square, with the main diagonal, it is the identity matrix: the matrix that changes nothing when you multiply by it, as 1 does for ordinary numbers.\n\nUse it as the starting point for a transformation you then modify, as the \"no change\" reference to compare a rotation or fit against, to add a little to a diagonal to steady an inversion, or (shifted off the main diagonal) as a matrix that moves each element one place along.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.eye <rows: int> <columns: int> <diagonal: int>\nDefaults: 3 rows, square, the main diagonal.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.eye 4\nnp.eye 3 4 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the matrix and sends it.\n\nrows (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many rows.\n\ncolumns (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many columns; 0 (the default) makes it square.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich diagonal holds the ones: 0 the main one, 1 the one just above it, -1 the one just below.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\neye out:\nThe matrix (32-bit floats).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nClicked at load: a 3 x 3 identity, and a 3 x 4 matrix with its ones one place to the right of the main diagonal. Change the numbers and click a button to see others.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diag makes a matrix with any values on the diagonal. t.eye is the PyTorch version. np.zeros and np.ones fill a whole matrix."
        },
